      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 The Authors. JPP © 2011 Royal Pharmaceutical Society.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "This study reports on the rapid isolation of verbascoside from Lippia citriodora H.B.K. (Verbenaceae), an inexpensive and widespread source, and the evaluation of its antihyperalgesic activity."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Isolation of verbascoside was achieved by size exclusion chromatography with Sephadex LH-20 eluting with 50% EtOH, which is proposed as a fast and efficient method of separation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "KEY FINDINGS",
            "content": "The antihyperalgesic activity of verbascoside was tested by in-vivo assay using the paw-pressure test in two animal models of neuropathic pain: a peripheral mononeuropathy produced either by a chronic constriction injury of the sciatic nerve (CCI) or by an intra-articular injection of sodium monoiodoacetate (MIA)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Verbascoside administered intraperitoneally at a dose of 100\u2003mg/kg reverted the mechanical hyperalgesia in both CCI and MIA treated rats, as evaluated in the paw-pressure test. Verbascoside was also effective against mechanical hyperalgesia after oral administration at doses of 300 and 600\u2003mg/kg."
          }
        ]
      },
